[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Delegates, Events, and Multicast Architecture in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Decoupling systems is a core requirement for scalable game architecture. Unreal Engine provides a robust event-driven programming model through Delegates and Events. This tutorial covers singlecast, multicast, dynamic delegates, and how to implement a multicast architecture."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Understanding Delegates and Events"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A delegate is a type-safe function pointer. It allows an object to invoke functions on other objects without knowing their concrete types. Unreal Engine categorizes delegates based on their threading, persistence, and binding capabilities:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Singlecast Delegates: Can bind to one function at a time."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Multicast Delegates: Can bind to multiple functions, allowing one-to-many communication."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic Delegates: Can be serialized and exposed to Blueprints, though with a slight performance cost."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Events: A restricted form of multicast delegate where only the owning class can trigger (broadcast) the event."},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"2. Declaring Native Delegates"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Native C++ delegates are extremely fast and should be used for high-performance internal systems. Below is an example of declaring a multicast delegate with parameters."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Declare a delegate that passes the damaging actor and the new health value
DECLARE_MULTICAST_DELEGATE_TwoParams(FOnHealthChangedSignature, AActor*, float);

UCLASS()
class ACharacterBase : public ACharacter
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    // Expose the delegate instance
    FOnHealthChangedSignature OnHealthChanged;
};"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"3. Implementing Events for Encapsulation"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent external classes from broadcasting your delegate (which breaks encapsulation), use the DECLARE_EVENT macro instead of a standard multicast delegate."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Declare an event tied to ACharacterBase
DECLARE_EVENT_TwoParams(ACharacterBase, FOnCharacterDiedEvent, AActor*, AController*);

UCLASS()
class ACharacterBase : public ACharacter
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    // Only ACharacterBase can call Broadcast() on this event
    FOnCharacterDiedEvent OnCharacterDied;
};"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"4. Binding and Broadcasting"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Other classes can subscribe to these delegates using the AddUObject method. Always ensure you safely unbind or manage object lifecycles to avoid dangling pointers."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"void UHealthUIComponent::InitializeComponent()
{
    Super::InitializeComponent();

    ACharacterBase* OwnerChar = Cast<ACharacterBase>(GetOwner());
    if (OwnerChar)
    {
        // Bind a member function to the multicast delegate
        OwnerChar->OnHealthChanged.AddUObject(this, &UHealthUIComponent::HandleHealthChanged);
    }
}

